<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hetman</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hetman was the title of the highest military commander in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, with separate grand and field hetmans for the Crown and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Hetmans held their posts for life and enjoyed considerable independence from the king. The office was a central feature of the Commonwealth's military organisation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hetman.jpg" alt="Hetman" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcello Bacciarelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Amor_Tarnowski.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Hetman is a title from Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, historically given to military commanders and comparable to a field marshal. The Polish spelling comes from the Old Czech hajtman, most likely composed of the German roots Haupt, meaning head, and Mann, meaning man. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> used it first, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussite-wars">Hussite Wars</a> of the fifteenth century onward. In the Principality of Moldavia and through much of the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, the hetman stood second in the army after the ruling prince, who held the position of voivode.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> the title Grand Crown Hetman dates from 1505. Until 1581 the post existed only during particular campaigns and wars; after that it became permanent, like the other offices of the Crown and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. The Commonwealth had four hetmans at any time: a great hetman and a field, or deputy, hetman each for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. From 1585 the title could not be taken away without a proven charge of treachery, so most hetmans served for life. Jan Karol Chodkiewicz commanded the army from his deathbed in 1621. Hetmans were not paid from the royal treasury.</p><p>Second only to the monarch in the army's chain of command, hetmans could not be removed by the king, which let them pursue independent policies. The arrangement worked well when a hetman was able and the monarch weak, but could produce disastrous results in the opposite case. It contrasted with neighbouring states, where sovereigns could dismiss their commanders at any time. A military reform of 1776 curtailed the hetmans' powers, and the office was abolished after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition of Poland</a> in 1795.</p><p>Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaporozhian-cossacks">Zaporozhian Cossacks</a>, commanders at the end of the sixteenth century were titled Koshovyi Otaman or Hetman. In 1648 the Zaporizhian Host elected a hetman of its own, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a>, whose uprising began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a>; its hetmans, though elected, held very broad powers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> abolished the office in 1764, ending with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrylo-rozumovsky">Kyrylo Rozumovsky</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The title was revived during the Ukrainian revolution of 1917 to 1921, when Pavlo Skoropadskyi, after a German-supported coup in early 1918, claimed the title Hetman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>; his regime lasted until late 1918. In the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> hejtman is the term for the elected governor of a region. The word has also come to mean commander or leader in general, and in Polish the chess queen is called hetman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hetman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After victories over the Poles, Bohdan Khmelnytsky entered Kyiv at Christmas 1648, hailed as a liberator from Polish captivity, and in February 1649 told a Polish delegation at Pereiaslav that he meant to be <b>hetman</b> of a Ruthenia stretching to Chelm and Halych.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The khans pursued their own policy in Eastern Europe: Islam III Giray allied with the Cossack <b>hetman</b> Bohdan Khmelnytsky in 1648, contributing to victories at Zhovti Vody and Korsun before abandoning him at Berestechko in 1651, and Mehmed IV Giray helped Hetman Ivan Vyhovsky defeat a Russian army…</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1569 the grand duchy kept its own army under a grand <b>hetman</b>, and in the seventeenth century its forces fought Sweden, Russia and the Cossacks, suffering a Russian occupation of Vilnius from 1655 to 1661.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The high point of Commonwealth arms came in 1610, when <b>Hetman</b> Stanisław Żółkiewski defeated a larger Russian and Swedish force at the Battle of Klushino and Polish troops occupied Moscow, where a group of boyars elected Sigismund III’s son Władysław, the future Władysław IV, as Tsar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His strategic recklessness, above all the invasion of Russia in 1708 and the decision to turn south into Ukraine in hope of support from the Cossack <b>hetman</b> Ivan Mazepa, led to the catastrophic defeat at Poltava (1709) that broke Swedish military power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It names military models: Dąbrowski, Napoleon, the 17th-century <b>hetman</b> Stefan Czarniecki and Kościuszko.</blockquote><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a></li></ul></section>
